WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.430
If a friend asked to borrow $100 from you,
would you do it?

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:09.780
You’re probably thinking, “That depends
on the friend.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:10.910
Are they trustworthy?

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.480
Have they borrowed money from me before, and
if so, did they pay it back?”

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.830
Borrowing money is as old as civilization
itself, and for a long time, it was based

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.370
on personal relationships and recommendations.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:34.030
The only way for a lender to know whether
they could trust someone to pay them back

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.480
was by knowing them personally, or if someone
else they already trusted vouched for them.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.809
But when credit became big business, lending
companies needed a more efficient way to decide

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.200
which borrowers they could trust.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.960
So, in 1956 a data analytics firm called Fair
Issac and Company started creating mathematical

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:55.469
models that used a borrower’s history to
measure their risk, and in 1989 they debuted

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:59.719
the first industry-standard algorithm: the
FICO score.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:04.199
This formula was soon adopted by the “big
three” credit reporting bureaus: Equifax,

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.950
Experian and Transunion.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:11.270
Your FICO score is a three-digit number between
300 and 850 that indicates to a lender how

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.110
safe it is to lend money to you.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.110
[show meter with more specific categories
like fair, exceptional, etc.]

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.439
A high score tells a lender that you’ll
probably pay the money back on time, so they’re

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:19.530
more likely to give you a loan with good terms.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.100
A low score signifies that you might not be
able to pay the money back, so if they’re

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.310
willing to offer you credit at all, they’ll
want to be compensated for the risk by charging

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.310
more interest.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:34.030
But it’s important to remember, this number
doesn’t represent who you are as a person,

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.600
or even whether you are financially responsible.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.939
There are people with high credit scores who
live paycheck to paycheck, and people with

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:44.310
low credit scores who save like little chipmunks.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.969
Your credit score only reflects your past
relationship to debt.

00:01:47.969 --> 00:01:51.539
Every time you borrow or repay money--like
taking out a credit card or making a student

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:55.020
loan payment--the lender reports that activity
to the credit bureau where it goes into the

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.810
math machine.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.280
But not all data is judged equally!

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.229
The largest percentage of your FICO score
is based on your payment history.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:06.060
So missing a payment is one of the worst things
you can do.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.840
But thankfully, late payment dings are like
broken bones.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.870
They may hurt for a while, but they eventually
heal, as long as you don’t break that same

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:14.870
bone again!

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.180
A close second is your credit to debt ratio.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:23.750
If you have a $1000 limit on your credit card
with a balance of $750, you’re using 75%

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.540
of your available credit.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.880
Hovering close to your limit is like driving
on empty--which looks risky to credit bureaus,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.310
so you’ll want to keep that ratio under
25%.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.140
That’s why paying down debt can really help
your score.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.280
The length of your credit history makes up
15% of your score.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.800
It’s based on the ages of your various accounts,
and it’s why older people often have higher

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.170
scores than younger people.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:53.570
It’s also why getting a credit card early--but
using it conservatively!--can help you have

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.410
a higher score later in life.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:00.950
10% of your score is based on credit diversity,
There are three main types of credit: revolving

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:05.350
(aka credit cards), installment, like student
loans and car loans, and mortgages.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.980
An ideal credit report has a mix of all three.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.810
The last 10% comes from new lines of credit
and credit checks, or “pulls.”

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:18.330
If you apply for a bunch of new credit cards
in a short period of time, or if many different

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.290
lenders have to check your score, that can
be a red flag.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:26.070
Though there are exceptions: if you’re shopping
for a car loan or a mortgage, you’ll usually

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:30.450
have a window of one or two months in which
multiple credit pulls won’t affect your

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.450
score much.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:36.920
Also, this only refers to pulls done by potential
creditors, called “hard” pulls.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.230
“Soft” pulls, by insurance companies or
employers won’t count against you.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.690
The FICO model is the main way your score is calculated.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.280
But there are many variations depending on what the lender

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.760
wants to know about you.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.220
And remember, your credit score is not the
be-all end-all of financial health--but there

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.390
are life goals that a good credit score can
make a lot easier to achieve, like owning

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.440
a house or starting a business.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.320
If you want to know what your score looks
like, many credit card and auto loan companies

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.410
provide it on your monthly statement.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:12.190
Alternately, the big three credit bureaus
are required by law to provide you with a

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:17.120
free credit report once a year--but they’ll
only calculate a score for a fee.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.460
And don’t worry, that’s considered a “soft
pull” and won’t hurt your score.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:25.010
It may seem a bit creepy that these faceless
organizations are monitoring your activities

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:29.380
in order to pass judgment on you, but it’s
really just our modern version of having a

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.120
good borrowing reputation.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.291
In which case, the same old rules still apply:
try to borrow as little as possible, and pay

00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:38.290
it back on time.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:39.840
And that’s our two cents!

